SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Freedom in the World 2023 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/bangladesh/freedom-world/2023","claim":"Freedom House says the 2022 local-election cycle was marred by allegations of fraud and the new Election Commission was selected without opposition participation, reflecting strong perceptions that the commission favored the ruling Awami League.","locator":"A3 Electoral Laws and Framework; paragraphs on EC selection and 2022 local elections","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary country report from a major democracy monitor with explicit 2022 coverage and specific institutional findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 politics and preparation for the 2024 national election cycle.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Hasina-era party control over election administration and candidate selection.","contrary_evidence":["The same report also notes that independent candidates and defectors defeated AL candidates in some local elections, so competition was not eliminated."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.4","4B.8","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Freedom in the World 2023 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/bangladesh/freedom-world/2023","claim":"Freedom House says BNP was able to organize major protests in 2022, though authorities and Awami League supporters disrupted some events, and that independent candidates and defectors defeated AL candidates in the 2021-22 local-election cycle.","locator":"B1/B2 paragraphs on opposition protests and local-election cycle","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary country report with explicit 2022 opposition-protest and local-election detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 and the immediate pre-election environment.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Hasina because it describes the government's handling of opposition space and local competition.","contrary_evidence":["This is a meaningful counterexample to total closure, because the opposition still managed some large protests and some non-AL candidates won locally."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.7","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Bangladesh","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/de/dokument/2089131.html","claim":"The executive summary says Bangladesh's constitution consolidates most power in the Office of the Prime Minister, that the 2018 election was not free and fair, and that 2022 saw serious restrictions on free expression, media, internet freedom, association, judicial independence, and political participation.","locator":"Executive Summary, lines 35-38","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary U.S. government country report mirrored in full text.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 and the inherited democratic baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Sheikh Hasina's government because it describes the governing structure and state practices under her administration.","contrary_evidence":["The report also says civilian authorities maintained effective control over the security forces, which is a limited counterweight to the abuse findings."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.4","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Freedom on the Net 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/bangladesh/freedom-net/2022","claim":"Freedom House says authorities frequently arrested online critics under the Digital Security Act, increased surveillance capacity, and backed a draft censorship law requiring traceability and long data retention.","locator":"Overview and Key Developments, lines 82-92","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary internet-freedom country report with explicit 2022 coverage and specific controls described.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2021-2022 internet-freedom period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Hasina's government because it concerns state censorship and arrest powers used against critics.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes that internet users mobilized online campaigns more actively than in previous years."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Freedom on the Net 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/bangladesh/freedom-net/2022","claim":"Freedom House says internet users mobilized online campaigns in 2022, reflecting a somewhat better environment for social-media activism than in earlier years.","locator":"Key Developments bullet on online mobilization, line 90","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same country report but a distinct, favorable 2022 claim about online mobilization.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2021-2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level context under Hasina's government; this is a partial counterpoint rather than proof of broad political openness.","contrary_evidence":["This positive point sits alongside the report's broader conclusion that internet freedom remained low."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN High Commissioner for Human Rights Michelle Bachelet concludes her official visit to Bangladesh","publisher":"United Nations Bangladesh / OHCHR","publication_date":"2022-08-17","url":"https://bangladesh.un.org/bn/node/196906","claim":"Bachelet said successive UN reports had documented narrowing civic space, increased surveillance, intimidation and reprisals that often led to self-censorship, and she urged stronger independent institutions including the elections commission and judiciary.","locator":"Press statement paragraphs on civic space and institutions, lines 70-73 and 103","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN press statement from the High Commissioner's 2022 visit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly on civic space.","ruler_attribution":"State-level criticism of Hasina's government; not a personal quote, but a direct official assessment of the governing environment.","contrary_evidence":["The same statement acknowledges Bangladesh's civil society strength and some women's participation gains, which slightly complicates a one-way decline narrative."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.4","4B.7","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN experts urge Bangladesh to end reprisals against human rights defenders and relatives of the disappeared","publisher":"OHCHR","publication_date":"2022-03-14","url":"https://www.ohchr.org/en/press-releases/2022/03/un-experts-urge-bangladesh-end-reprisals-against-human-rights-defenders-and","claim":"UN experts said Bangladeshi authorities launched threats, intimidation and harassment against relatives of forcibly disappeared persons and human-rights defenders after U.S. sanctions on RAB officials.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and the summary of the experts' concerns","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN human-rights press release on a dated 2022 incident pattern.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Hasina's government because the reprisals were alleged against state actors and were triggered by the government's response to sanctions.","contrary_evidence":["The statement describes allegations and intimidation patterns, not a formal adjudication of every individual incident."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.9","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Freedom on the Net 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/bangladesh/freedom-net/2022","claim":"Freedom House also says online mobilization remained more viable in 2022 than in prior years, indicating some residual civic space despite the broader crackdown.","locator":"Key Developments bullet on online mobilization","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same primary country report, but this is a distinct favorable claim about online organizing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2021-2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level context rather than a ruler credit claim; it is a narrow counterpoint to the broader repression record.","contrary_evidence":["This does not offset the report's broader finding that authorities continued to arrest critics and expand surveillance powers."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Authorities must end repression of demonstrations","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/12/bangladesh-authorities-must-end-repression-of-demonstrations/","claim":"Amnesty said the authorities were escalating a crackdown on demonstrations, with about 1,400 political activists arrested and more than 60 people injured in the 7 December Dhaka clashes, and called for an end to excessive force and impunity.","locator":"Background section and the quoted regional director statement","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights-organization statement tied to a specific 2022 protest crackdown.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Bangladesh authorities under Hasina's government; the report is on state response, not isolated local misconduct.","contrary_evidence":["The article relies on media reports for the casualty count, so the exact numbers remain partly contingent."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh: Crackdown on Political Opposition","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-10-10","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/10/10/bangladesh-crackdown-political-opposition","claim":"HRW reported that since late August 2022 at least four people had died and hundreds had been injured in clashes involving police, BNP supporters, and ruling-party supporters, while police carried out mass arrests of opposition supporters and ruling-party affiliates appeared to enjoy impunity for attacks.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and the Banani candlelight-vigil incident description","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous rights-monitor reporting with specific incident detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Hasina's government because it concerns police conduct and ruling-party-aligned impunity during opposition protests.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes that each side accused the other of instigating violence, so not every clash is exclusively state-caused."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}

